WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.899
The German company&nbsp;BioNTech&nbsp;partnered with
Pfizer to develop and test a coronavirus vaccine

00:00:05.899 --> 00:00:12.969
known as&nbsp;BNT162b2. A clinical trial demonstrated
that the vaccine has an&nbsp;efficacy rate&nbsp;of

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:16.990
95 percent in preventing Covid-19.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:23.400
The SARS-CoV-2 virus is studded with proteins
that it uses to enter human cells. These so-called

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.750
spike proteins make a tempting target for
potential vaccines and treatments. Like the

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:34.260
Moderna vaccine, the Pfizer-BioNTech vaccine
is based on the virus’s genetic instructions

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:40.750
for building the spike protein.
The vaccine uses messenger RNA, genetic material

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:45.910
that our cells read to make proteins. The
molecule — called mRNA for short — is

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:50.640
fragile and would be chopped to pieces by
our natural enzymes if it were injected directly

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:57.320
into the body. To protect their vaccine, Pfizer
and BioNTech wrap mRNA in oily bubbles made

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:03.780
of lipid nanoparticles. Because of their fragility,
the mRNA molecules will quickly fall apart

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:09.780
at room temperature. Pfizer is building special
containers with dry ice, thermal sensors and

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:15.490
GPS trackers to ensure the vaccines can be
transported at -94 degrees Fahrenheit to stay

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:17.810
viable.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:23.920
After injection, the vaccine particles bump
into cells and fuse to them, releasing mRNA.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:29.920
The cell’s molecules read its sequence and
build spike proteins. The mRNA from the vaccine

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:35.710
is eventually destroyed by the cell, leaving
no permanent trace. Some of the spike proteins

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:41.360
form spikes that migrate to the surface of
the cell and stick out their tips. The vaccinated

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:47.090
cells also break up some of the proteins into
fragments, which they present on their surface.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:51.310
These protruding spikes and spike protein
fragments can then be recognized by the immune

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.520
system.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.789
When a vaccinated cell dies, the debris will
contain many spike proteins and protein fragments,

00:01:58.789 --> 00:02:04.039
which can then be taken up by a type of immune
cell called an antigen-presenting cell. The

00:02:04.039 --> 00:02:09.220
cell presents fragments of the spike protein
on its surface. When other cells called helper

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:14.110
T-cells detect these fragments, the helper
T-cells can raise the alarm and help marshal

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:17.970
other immune cells to fight the infection.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:23.070
Other immune cells, called B-cells, may bump
into the coronavirus spikes and protein fragments

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:28.720
on the surface of vaccinated cells. A few
of the B-cells may be able to lock onto the

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:34.180
spike proteins. If these B-cells are then
activated by helper T-cells, they will start

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:39.450
to proliferate and pour out antibodies that
target the spike protein.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.070
The antibodies can latch onto coronavirus
spikes, mark the virus for destruction and

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:49.180
prevent infection by blocking the spikes from
attaching to other cells.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:53.650
The antigen-presenting cells can also activate
another type of immune cell called a killer

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:59.390
T-cell to seek out and destroy any coronavirus-infected
cells that display the spike protein fragments

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:05.250
on their surfaces.
The Pfizer-BioNTech vaccine requires two injections,

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:11.470
given 21 days apart, to prime the immune system
well enough to fight off the coronavirus.

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:15.720
But because the vaccine is so new, researchers
don’t know how long its protection might

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:21.030
last. It’s possible that in the months after
vaccination, the number of antibodies and

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:26.820
killer T-cells will drop. But the immune system
also contains special cells called memory

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:31.550
B-cells and memory T-cells that might retain
information about the coronavirus for years

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:32.610
or even decades.

